\title{Constant coefficients}
\taxon{Example}
\date{2021-10-01}
\author{JB00}
\import{macros}

\p{#{5\frac{dy}{dx} - 3y = 0}. Try #{y = Ae^{\lambda x}}, #{\frac{dy}{dx} = \lambda Ae^{\lambda x}}. The ODE #{\Rightarrow} #{(5\lambda - 3) = 0} (“characteristic equation”), so #{\lambda = \frac{3}{5}}. So #{y = Ae^{3x/5}} holds for all #{A} since this is a linear, homogeneous equation.}

\ul{
\li{Generally, for any linear homogeneous ODE (not necessarily first order), any constant multiple of a solution is a solution.}
\li{An #{n^{\text{th}}} order linear ODE has exactly #{n} independent solutions.}
}

\p{So #{y = Ae^{3x/5}} is \em{the} general solution.}
